// Case studies (user request, 2026-09-29): real North Pacific events of the
// past year, walked through step by step in the Past year viewer. Each step
// sets the map (the Past year's view, anomaly or value, lines and wind), opens
// its day, points the camera, and optionally pins the probe or offers to play
// a run of days; its text says what to look at and why.
//
// Every number in the texts comes from the archive's own GFS analyses (the
// 00 UTC analysis of the day named, 1 degree grid) unless the text credits
// another source; sources are listed per case. The archive rolls forward a
// day at a time, so a case whose days have left it is shown as no longer
// available rather than opened on the wrong days.
//
// Pure, no imports: node --test loads it. The texts are HTML (trusted: they're
// written here, not taken from data).

// A case: {id, region (pnw | hawaii | pacific, the list's groups), title, when,
// where, summary, sources, check (one question across the steps, "question? (answer)", shown under the last step's
// recap; panel review 2026-10-14), steps}. A step: {title, text, why (why this map), lookFor (what to find on it), day: "YYYY-MM-DD", show: {view, mode, lines, wind,
// windAs}, look: {lat, lon, span (deg of latitude in view), tilt (deg from
// straight down, 0-60)}, probe: [lat, lon] | undefined, play: [fromDay,
// toDay] | undefined, marks: [{lat, lon, label, r km}] (rings and labels on
// the map at the features the text names) | undefined, takeaway: the step's point in one sentence (shown after Look for), hrrr: {from, to: "YYYY-MM-DDTHH" UTC, every: hours, view
// (hrrr.js HRRR_VIEWS, or "o_" + an obsimg.js kind to open on an observation), hint: what to watch in it} | undefined
// (the high-resolution hours: HRRR 3 km in the Northwest, tools/fetch_case_hrrr.py; RTMA 2.5 km in Hawaiʻi,
// tools/fetch_case_rtma.py; with radar and satellite, tools/fetch_case_obsimg.py)}.
// Northwest and Hawaiʻi cases also carry patterns (the weather patterns they show, keys of PATTERNS, each card with
// its region; user request
// 2026-10-14) and a season (meteorological: Dec-Feb winter, and so on; "several" for a case spanning
// seasons), so the list can be read as a pattern library. Each pattern has a card: when it happens, its
// signature aloft and at the surface, the forecasters' numbers that pick it out (pnwreading.js) and the
// maps that show it best. The cards summarise NWS Seattle, UW and Washington State Climate Office
// climatology; numbers are rules of thumb, not statistics.
