    check: `Why did the 951 hPa low raise such big seas, and why were they biggest on its southern side? (It was far deeper than a usual winter low, so its isobars were packed and its winds strong, and on its southern side that wind blew the same way for longest, building the biggest waves.)`,
    steps: [
      { title: "A parade of lows", takeaway: "In winter, lows form off Japan and Kamchatka and run north-east along the storm track, one after another.", day: "2026-01-03", show: P("mslp", "actual", "isobars", "10"), look: { lat: 52, lon: 180, span: 30, tilt: 15 }, play: ["2026-01-02", "2026-01-06"], marks: [{ lat: 56, lon: 170, label: "Low, 966 hPa" }, { lat: 51, lon: 159, label: "990 hPa" }, { lat: 57, lon: 155, label: "990 hPa" }],
        why: `Sea-level pressure with the 10 m wind shows the storms and their winds; playing the days shows them moving along the storm track.`,
        lookFor: `the lows lined up from Kamchatka to the Gulf of Alaska; each one moving north-east as you play.`,
        text: `<p>On 3 January a 966 hPa low sits at 56°N 170°E, with others lined up behind it along the storm track. The Ocean Prediction Center warned of a hurricane-force low in the Bering Sea that day (winds of 64 kt or more; ESSIC).</p><p>Press <b>Play these days</b>: lows form off Japan and Kamchatka and run north-east into the Bering Sea and the Gulf of Alaska, one after another.</p><p><b>Ask:</b> If you follow one low as you play, where would you expect it a few days later? (Further north-east, in the Bering Sea or the Gulf of Alaska, with the next low following behind it.)</p>` },
      { title: "951 hPa", takeaway: "The closer the isobars round a low, the stronger the wind, and this one was far deeper than a usual winter low.", day: "2026-01-05", show: P("mslp", "anomaly", "isobars", "10"), look: { lat: 52, lon: 185, span: 30, tilt: 15 }, marks: [{ lat: 55, lon: 181, label: "Low, 951 hPa (\u221248)" }],
        why: `The pressure anomaly shows how far below normal the storm is, which makes it stand out from the usual winter lows.`,
        lookFor: `the deep-blue anomaly at the low's centre; the tightly packed isobars around it.`,
        text: `<p>By the 5th a low of 951 hPa sits at 55°N 179°W, 48 hPa below normal for the date. Its isobars are packed tight: the closer the isobars, the stronger the wind.</p><p><b>Ask:</b> Where around this low would you expect the strongest wind? (Where the isobars are packed tightest, close around its centre.)</p>` },
      { title: "The sea state", takeaway: "The biggest waves form where strong wind blows the same way for longest, here on the storm's southern side.", day: "2026-01-05", show: P("hs", "actual", "isobars", "10"), look: { lat: 50, lon: 185, span: 30, tilt: 15 }, marks: [{ lat: 51, lon: 186, label: "Seas 13.4 m", r: 200 }],
        why: `Wave height shows what the wind does to the sea; the biggest waves need strong wind blowing a long way.`,
        lookFor: `the biggest waves on the storm's southern side; where the waves go on the following days (step on).`,
        text: `<p>Significant wave height (GFS-Wave): 13.4 m at 51°N 174°W, among the biggest mid-latitude seas of the year. The biggest waves are on the storm's southern side, where the wind blows the same way for longest (the <b>fetch</b>).</p><p><b>Ask:</b> step to the 6th and 7th. Where do the waves go after the wind drops? (They travel on as swell, south-east toward Hawaiʻi and the West Coast; switch to wave period and look for the long periods.)</p>` },
    ],
